# Chapter 2: File.  The chapter is kept in four sources that this file reads:
# the concepts (file0), the templates, backups and revisions (file1), the
# commands Save and Retrieve (file2) and the commands New, Delete, Import
# and Export, the messages and the examples (file3).
.doc Strata Reference: File
.chapter 2
.h1 File [file]
.ix File menu
.ix Files, chapter overview
.ix F12, File
.ix Disk files
A Strata document is stored in a file on the disk.  The File menu holds the
commands that move documents between the Editor and the disk: Save writes the
document, Retrieve reads one, New starts a new document, Delete removes a
file, and Import and Export exchange plain text with other programs.  All six
commands have a panel of the same kind and follow the same rules for names,
directories and checklists.

Strata uses the file system of the operating system: a file is a name in a
directory, a directory is reached by a path, and a path that begins with a
slash starts at the root.  Strata adds a few conventions: the extension that
a command gives to a name that has none, the checklists that show what is in
a directory, the backup file, the template, and the revision number of a
document.

.h2 How the chapter is organized [file-organization]
The chapter describes the shared objects first and the commands second,
because the six commands share most of their rules.
.table 19 41
Part | Sections
The objects that the commands work on | {X:file-types} names the three
 | types of file.  {X:file-workdir} describes the working directory that
 | every panel shows first, {x:file-names} the rules for file names, and
 | {x:file-checklist} the lists that the key {k:F2} opens.  {X:file-panels}
 | gives the keys that are valid in every File panel, so that the entries
 | of the commands need not repeat them.
The things that a document carries | {X:file-templates}, {x:file-backup}
 | and {x:file-revision}.
The commands, in the order of the menu | {X:file-save}, {x:file-retrieve},
 | {x:file-new}, {x:file-delete}, {x:file-import} and {x:file-export}.
 | Each begins with the ways to reach it and its panel, and ends with the
 | messages and the borderline cases of that command.
Reference and practice | {X:file-messages} lists the messages of the File
 | commands, and {x:file-examples} gives a few worked examples.
.end
The File commands belong to the Editor.  The Document Manager opens a
document with its own command, Edit / Preview / Print Document
({n:str-edit}), and starts the Editor on the file; from then on the commands
of this chapter apply.

